{"availability":"available","full":"missyprs/COMPX341-A3","name":"COMPX341-A3","description":"A working version of the aws-bookstore-demo-app. Used in COMPX341-22A Assignment 3. Original Repository can be found: https://github.com/aws-samples/aws-bookstore-demo-app","category":"Other","language":"TypeScript","stars":0,"forks":45,"created":"2022-05-01T23:17:42Z","updated":"2022-05-10T22:32:10Z","license":"MIT-0","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":"https://d2h3ljlsmzojxz.cloudfront.net/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.751Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.751Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.751Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.751Z","stars":0,"forks":45}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["AWS Bookstore Demo App is a full-stack sample web application that creates a storefront (and backend) for customers to shop for fictitious books. The entire application can be created with a single CloudFormation template. Try out the deployed application here!","You can browse and search for books, look at recommendations and best sellers, manage your cart, checkout, view your orders, and more. Get started with building your own below!","The goal of AWS Bookstore Demo App is to provide a fully-functional web application that utilizes multiple purpose-built AWS databases and native AWS components like Amazon API Gateway and AWS CodePipeline. Increasingly, modern web apps are built using a multitude of different databases. Developers break their large applications into individual components and select the best database for each job. Let's consider AWS Bookstore Demo App as an example. The app contains multiple experiences such a shopping cart, product search, recommendations, and a top sellers list. For each of these use cases,…"],"features":["Product catalog/shopping cart - Amazon DynamoDB offers fast, predictable performance for the key-value lookups needed in the product catalog, as well as the shopping cart and order history. In this implementation, we…","Search - Amazon Elasticsearch Service enables full-text search for our storefront, enabling users to find products based on a variety of terms including author, title, and category.","Recommendations - Amazon Neptune provides social recommendations based on what user's friends have purchased, scaling as the storefront grows with more products, pages, and users.","Top sellers list - Amazon ElastiCache for Redis reads order information from Amazon DynamoDB Streams, creating a leaderboard of the “Top 20” purchased or rated books.","Serverless service backend – Amazon API Gateway powers the interface layer between the frontend and backend, and invokes serverless compute with AWS Lambda."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"2f84f01a761abe228f0572a4da7e466c5ab497b02a51edbc64e45137e190c121","extractedAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.876Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.876Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"AWS Bookstore Demo App is a full-stack sample web application that creates a storefront (and backend) for customers to shop for fictitious books. The entire application can be created with a single CloudFormation template. Try out the deployed application here!","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[{"kind":"account","status":"required","text":"Amazon Cognito handles user account creation and login for the bookstore application.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md"},{"kind":"account","status":"required","text":"For the purposes of the demo, the bookstore is only available to browse after login, which could represent the architecture of different types of web apps.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md"},{"kind":"paid_service","status":"optional","text":"If you want to maintain low latency for your app, this deeplink creates an identical stack but with additional triggers to keep the Lamdba functions \"warm.\" Given that these triggers make the Lamdba functions run more frequently (every 10 minutes, on a schedule), this will add a small amount to the overall cost to run the application.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md"}],"fingerprint":"2f84f01a761abe228f0572a4da7e466c5ab497b02a51edbc64e45137e190c121","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.877Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2022-05-10T22:32:10Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/missyprs/COMPX341-A3/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-10T01:09:08.879Z","hash":"2f84f01a761abe228f0572a4da7e466c5ab497b02a51edbc64e45137e190c121","originalBytes":31551,"storedBytes":31551,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["aws","amazon","nbsp","application","string","demo","lambda","web","books","dynamodb","app","bookstore","number","table","cart","function","orders","user","template","create","cloudformation","search","bookid","get","key","price","functions","stack","users","elasticsearch","application.","full-stack","recommendations","these","used","which","api","book","cloudfront","customerid","elasticache","purposes","resources","run","see","account","assets","category","choose","components","created","list","quantity","redis","sellers","app.","backend","best","build","code","codebuild","cognito","different","gateway","name","neptune","new","partition","purchased","top","when","available","awslambdabasicexecutionrole","bucket","checkout","codepipeline","etc.","frontend","only","order","own","primary","provided","updates","via","architecture","bookdetail","cart.","codecommit","console","credentials","developer","diagram","friends","iam","information","launch","like","make","once","provide","social","there","what","where","add","after","author","ba